<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Belshazzar</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. c. 539 BCE</span> · <a href="/centuries/6th-century-bce">6th century BCE</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Belshazzar was the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nabonidus">Nabonidus</a>, the last king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/chaldean-empire">Neo-Babylonian Empire</a>, and governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/babylon">Babylon</a> as regent for about a decade while his father lived at the Arabian oasis of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tayma">Tayma</a>. In the Book of Daniel he is the king who sees the writing on the wall at a feast on the night Babylon falls. He probably died when the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persians">Persians</a> took the city in 539 BCE.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/belshazzar.jpg" alt="Belshazzar" width="320" height="308" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nabonidus_chronicle.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
